"Signals: Evolution, Learning, and Information | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/3092)\nciteturn23search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199580828.001.0001 ... 9780199580828 ... Oxford University Press\n\n# Signals: Evolution, Learning, and Information\n\n[Input: Search in this book] Search in this book\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199580828.001.0001\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780191722769\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780199580828\n\nPublisher:\n\nOxford University Press\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\n[Button: Disclosure]\n\nAs an Amazon Associate OUP earns from qualifying purchases.\n\nBook\n\n# Signals: Evolution, Learning, and Information\n\nBrian Skyrms\n\nBrian Skyrms\n\nStanford University and the University of California, Irvine\n\nFind on\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nAuthor Webpage\n\nPublished:\n\n8 April 2010\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199580828.001.0001\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780191722769\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780199580828\n\nPublisher:\n\nOxford University Press\n\n[Input: Search in this book] Search in this book\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\n[Button: Disclosure]\n\nAs an Amazon Associate OUP earns from qualifying purchases.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis book presents an exploration of how fundamental signals are to our world. It uses a variety of tools — theories of signaling games, information, evolution, and learning — to investigate how meaning and communication develop. The book shows how signaling games themselves evolve, and introduces a new model of learning with invention. The juxtaposition of atomic signals leads to complex signals, as the natural product of gradual process. Signals operate in networks of senders and receivers at all levels of life. Information is transmitted, but it is also processed in various ways. That is how we think — signals run around a very complicated signaling network. Signaling is a key ingredient in the evolution of teamwork, in the human but also in the animal world, even in micro-organisms. Communication and co-ordination of action are different aspects of the flow of information, and are both effected by signals.\n\nKeywords: signals, signaling games, information, evolution, learning, meaning, communication, invention, senders, receivers\n\nSubject\n\nPhilosophy of Science Epistemology Philosophy of Language\n\nContents\n\n  * ### Front Matter\n\n    * Copyright Page\n    * Dedication\n    * Acknowledgments\n  * Introduction\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 1 1 Signals\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 2 2 Signals in Nature\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 3 3 Information\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 4 4 Evolution\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 5 5 Evolution in Lewis Signaling Games\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 6 6 Deception\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 7 7 Learning\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 8 8 Learning in Lewis Signaling Games\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 9 9 Generalizing Signaling Games: Synonyms, Bottlenecks, Category Formation\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 10 10 Inventing New Signals\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 11 11 Networks I: Logic and Information Processing\n\nView chapter\n\n  * 12 12 Complex Signals and Compositionality\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 13 13 Networks II: Teamwork\n\nView chapter\n\n  * Expand 14 14 Learning to Network\n\nView chapter\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCellular Automata and Fractal Urban Form: A Cellular Modelling Approach to the Evolution of Urban Land-Use Patterns - R White, G Engelen, 1993 (https://doi.org/10.1068/A251175)\nciteturn23search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 33.3 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Cellular Automata and Fractal Urban Form: A Cellular Modelling Approach to the Evolution of Urban Land-Use Patterns ... https://doi.org/10.1068/a251175 ... In this paper, a cellular automaton is developed to model the spatial structure of urban land use over time.\n\nResearch article\n\nFirst published August 1993\n\nRe-use permissions\n\n# Cellular Automata and Fractal Urban Form: A Cellular Modelling Approach to the Evolution of Urban Land-Use Patterns\n\nR White and G EngelenView all authors and affiliations\n\nVolume 25, Issue 8\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1068/a251175\n\n## Abstract\n\nCellular automata belong to a family of discrete, connectionist techniques being used to investigate fundamental principles of dynamics, evolution, and self-organization. In this paper, a cellular automaton is developed to model the spatial structure of urban land use over time. For realistic parameter values, the model produces fractal or bifractal land-use structures for the urbanized area and for each individual land-use type. Data for a set of US cities show that they have very similar fractal dimensions. The cellular approach makes it possible to achieve a high level of spatial detail and realism and to link the results directly to general theories of structural evolution.\n\n## References\n\nAllen P, 1983, “Self-organization and evolution in human systems”, in Cities and Regions as Nonlinear Decision Systems Ed. Crosby R (Westview Press, Boulder, CO) pp 29–62\n\nAllen P, Sanglier M, 1979, “A dynamical model of growth in a central place system” Geographical Analysis 11 256–272\n\nAllen P, Engelen G, Sanglier M, 1984, “Self-organizing dynamic models of human systems”, in Macroscopic to Microscopic Order (Synergetics, Volume 22) Ed. Ferhland E (Springer, Berlin) pp 150–171\n\nBak P, Chen K, 1989, “The physics of fractals” Physica D 38 5–12\n\nBak P, Chen K, Creutz M, 1989, “Self-organized criticality in the ‘game of life’” Nature (London) 342 780–782\n\nBarbe A, 1990, “A cellular automaton ruled by an eccentric conservation law” Physica D 45 49–62\n\nBatty M, 1991a, “Cities as fractals: Simulating growth and form”, in Fractals and Chaos Eds Crilly AJ, Earnshaw RA, Jones H (Springer, New York) pp 43–69\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEa