ll levels of biological organization. Monkeys, birds, bees, and even bacteria have signaling systems. Multicellular organisms are only possible because internal signals coordinate the actions of their constituents. This chapter addresses two main questions: How can interacting individuals spontaneously learn to signal? How can species spontaneously evolve signalling systems? It discusses how we can bring contemporary theoretical tools to bear on these questions.\n\nKeywords: signals, signaling systems, senders, receivers\n\nSubject\n\nPhilosophy of Science Epistemology Philosophy of Language\n\nCollection: Oxford Scholarship Online--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCellular automata and fractal urban form: a cellular modelling approach to the evolution of urban land-use patterns (https://pascal-francis.inist.fr/vibad/index.php?action=getRecordDetail&idt=6076477)\nciteturn23search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 33.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Cellular automata and fractal urban form: a cellular modelling approach to the evolution of urban land-use patterns ...     Spatial analysis Self-organizing behaviour Urban growth Spatial dynamics Urban area Fractal geometry Urban model Simulation Structural stability Urban structure Spatial system Land use ... Image DOI  10.1068/a251175\n\n# Cellular automata and fractal urban form: a cellular modelling approach to the evolution of urban land-use patterns\n\nAutor\nFuente\n    \n\nEnvironment & planning A (Print). 1993, Vol 25, Num 8, pp 1175-1199 ; Illustration ; ref : 54 réf\n\nISSN\n    0308-518X\nCampo Científico\n    Geography\nEditor\n    Pion, London\n\nPaís de la publicación\n    United Kingdom\nTipo de documento\n    Article\n\nIdioma\n    English\nPalabra clave (fr)\n    Analyse spatiale Auto-organisation Croissance urbaine Dynamique spatiale Espace urbain Géométrie fractale Modèle urbain Simulation Stabilité structurale Structure urbaine Système spatial Utilisation